#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The root-owned half of Builder's install.

    sudo <absolute python3> -I /opt/builder/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/install-privileged \
        --shape two --org <name> [--release <tag>] [--agent-platform-repo <name>] \
        [--shipwright-repo <name>] [--library-repo <name>] [--convivy-orient-repo <name>] \
        [--commit <repo>=<sha>]...
    sudo <absolute python3> -I /opt/builder/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/install-privileged \
        --shape three --org <name> [--release <tag>] [--agent-platform-repo <name>] \
        [--shipwright-repo <name>] [--library-repo <name>] [--convivy-orient-repo <name>] \
        [--commit <repo>=<sha>]... [--consent ask|yes|no]
    sudo /opt/builder/libexec/update-privileged

`--shape two` installs the root half that holds all four repositories: the bare
copy, the extracted tree and the install record for each of shipwright,
agent-platform, library and convivy-orient, plus the settings and the entry
point. It fetches each anonymously over HTTPS from the organisation
`install.json` records, taking the release by tag — named by `--release`, or the
tag a prior install of this shape recorded, when `--release` is not given — except
that convivy-orient falls back to a token minted from the Orient read key
(`etc/orient-read-app.pem`, placed with `github-gate --place-key --orient-read`)
when an anonymous probe of it answers 401, which happens only when an
install's organisation keeps that one repository private. Naming `--commit
<repo>=<sha>` for one or more of the four repositories checks each against
that repository's own tag before anything is fetched from it, exactly as
shape three's own `--commit` does; a repository named with no `--commit`
fetches the tag with no such check, and naming one with no `--release` to
check it against is refused. It places `libexec/model-provider` from
agent-platform's tree, and on a Mac the pinned Python shape three also
places. It places no review launcher, no sudo rule, no approver key and no
reviewers' tools.

`--shape three` installs the three-account root half: the review launcher, the
reviewers' tools, the one sudo rule that reaches the launcher, and a root-owned
tree and record line for library and for convivy-orient. Nothing yet reads either tree.

**This runs only from its own extracted tree.** It refuses unless its file sits at
`<prefix>/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/install-privileged` and every directory from the
prefix down, plus the file and `github-gate` beside it, belongs to the account
running it with no group or other write bit. The first run has the same shape, and root
does the extraction, so the tree belongs to root:

    sudo mkdir -p /opt/builder/src/shipwright@<sha>
    git -C <checkout> archive <sha> | sudo tar -x -C /opt/builder/src/shipwright@<sha>
    sudo <absolute python3> -I /opt/builder/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/install-privileged --shape two ...

A tree the operator's own account extracted is refused, because its files belong to that
account. The check runs before `github-gate` is loaded, because `github-gate` sits beside
this file.

**Root installs only a commit confirmed by a signed release, or by GitHub's own `main`.**
With a release tag — named by `--release`, or the tag a prior anonymous install of this
shape recorded, read when `--release` is not given — root fetches shipwright,
agent-platform, library and convivy-orient anonymously over HTTPS, each by that tag,
following a four-step order (ls-remote, recorded-tag check, fetch without force, peel
and ancestry) so a tag moved backward, or a recorded tag moved forward, is refused. On
the two-account shape, convivy-orient alone falls back to a token minted from the Orient
read key when one anonymous HTTPS probe of it (a GET of its `info/refs`, the same request
`git` makes to learn whether a remote takes the smart protocol) answers 401, which happens
only when that install's organisation keeps convivy-orient private; the other three
repositories are always anonymous. A probe that answers neither 200 nor 401 — another
status, a redirect, or a connection that fails outright — refuses rather than guessing,
naming what the probe saw. On the two-account shape, `--commit <repo>=<sha>` is accepted
for any of the four repositories, each checked against the tag's own commit before
anything is fetched from that repository; a mismatch is refused and chooses nothing, and
so is a `--commit` given with no `--release`, since a plain replay carries no tag to
check it against. On the three-account shape, naming a tag with `--release` instead
requires `--commit` for every one of the four repositories, checked the same way. The
two-account shape always takes the tag path. With no tag at all — the three-account
shape's only other case — root instead mints
a read token from the approver key for each repository, fetches `refs/heads/main` alone
into a bare copy under `<prefix>/src`, and revokes the token; the commit it installs is
the fetched head, or the one named with `--commit <repo>=<sha>`, refused unless it is an
ancestor of that fetched `main`. Each commit is extracted with `git archive` into its own
root-owned tree, refused if it holds a symbolic link, a submodule or any other special
entry. Everything placed comes from those trees alone, read without following a link.
Nothing under the `agents` home or a deploy area is read, except the instruction-file diff
that the operator is shown and may consent to on the three-account shape.

**Nothing live changes until everything is built.** The launcher, library, sudo
rule, reviewers' tools and venv are staged beside their live paths and checked. Only
then are they swapped in, and the entry point is rewritten last.

**The entry point runs root's own copy.** `libexec/update-privileged` takes no
arguments. It runs the copy in the newest tree it has installed, under `env -i`. A
newer shipwright commit is installed by running that commit's copy, and the entry
point moves only when that copy succeeds.

**Both shapes place the deploy step's keyring and entry point.** GitHub's web-flow
public key, shipped as `payload/etc/github-web-flow.asc`, goes to
`etc/github-web-flow.asc`, and `libexec/orient-deploy` is rewritten to run the newest
tree's `orient-deploy`. Neither does anything until a person turns on following
Orient's `main`.

**Root keeps the root half it replaces, and puts it back with no download.**
Each run that places a new root half first saves the record it is about to
replace as `src/installed.previous`, in the same staged swap as the new
record, so a kill before the swap leaves neither written and a kill after
leaves both. Nothing removes a tree from `src` once extracted, so the old
release's trees are still on the machine.

    sudo <absolute python3> -I /opt/builder/src/shipwright@<old sha>/bin/install-privileged \
        --shape two|three --restore-previous

`--restore-previous` reinstalls, from the trees `installed.previous` names, checked to
be root-owned, what each shape itself places: on shape three, the review launcher, the
model-provider tool, root's own copy of agent-platform's library and the sudo rule; on
shape two, the model-provider tool alone, since shape two places none of the rest. Both
shapes restore the web-flow keyring and rewrite both entry points to that shipwright
tree. It fetches nothing and makes no git call: the reviewers' own venv, Claude binary
and instruction file, which only a network fetch rebuilds, are left as they are. It
refuses, changing nothing, when no saved record exists, a tree it names is missing, or
one is not root-owned. It is the only way root's half moves to an older commit, and only
the administrator's `sudo` starts it.

**Settings persist, and only the settings that name this install do.** The first
run writes `install.json` beside the slots: the organisation, the repository names
(shipwright, agent-platform, library and convivy-orient) and the shape.
An `entities` list an operator hand-places there carries forward byte-for-byte, on
either shape, and so does `follow_orient_main`, which only `orient-deploy --enable`
writes. `approver_app_id` is different: every run derives it fresh from the
root-only id slot `github-gate --place-key` fills once GitHub has vouched for
the key, that slot being the single source, since a key may be placed before
install.json exists at all; where no id is placed, the value already in
install.json carries forward byte-for-byte instead. On a multi-entity install,
the three-account shape also derives an `accounts` map from `entities` fresh on
every run, for the review launcher's own use. Test settings never persist.
They come from `fixtures.json` under the prefix, and only where the prefix is
not the real install root.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

